research_rationale:
Text and identity: read in full in the registered American Presidency Project transcription (jobs/app-executive-orders/output/texts/executive-order-8844-amending-executive-order-no-8827-july-24-1941-exempting-harry-t.txt; grade mirror_transcription, acceptable_as_original false; standing note: original not yet held; replace and re-check quotes when an official copy is retrieved). Page check per jobs/claim-kit/AGENT_PROTOCOL.md (working-text paragraph), done 2026-11-04: the APP mirror page record with the same sha256 in jobs/app-executive-orders/output/MIRROR_DOCUMENTS.jsonl (Common Crawl capture 2024-11-12T13:52:37Z) has the heading "Executive Order 8844—Amending Executive Order No. 8827 of July 24, 1941, Exempting Harry T. Edwards From Compulsory Retirement for Age", its date (1941-08-08) and subject agree with the held text, and its text_sha256 equals the held file's. Identity: the register lists this number with date 1941-08-08, signer Franklin D. Roosevelt and the title "Amendment of Exemption of Harry T. Edwards From Compulsory Retirement for Age"; the held text amends EO 8827 of July 24, 1941 concerning Harry T. Edwards and ends with the signature "FRANKLIN D ROOSEVELT" and the date line "August 8, 1941.". The register title (Amendment of Exemption of Harry T. Edwards...) and the APP heading (Amending Executive Order No. 8827 ...) describe the same instrument. Clauses are quoted from that registered file, not from the unregistered clause-drafts copy.
Content: the order is one sentence, with no recital. Acting under section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), it provides that Executive Order No. 8827, which exempted Harry T. Edwards (senior technologist, Bureau of Plant Industry, Department of Agriculture) "from compulsory retirement for age for a period of one year ending October 31, 1941", "is hereby amended so as to exempt Mr. Edwards from compulsory retirement for a period of one year ending October 31, 1942". The inventory is one clause, which both amends EO 8827's period and states the exemption period (one year ending October 31, 1942). Reading chosen and the alternative: the clause's life is the exemption it grants, so its end is the last day of that period, October 31, 1942, which the sentence states. The alternative reading treats the amending command as performed when the order was signed on 1941-08-08 and leaves only its consequence (the changed period in EO 8827); that reading would not change when the exemption ended. The amended order, EO 8827, is in this packet; its own claim records this order as a later event and uses the same last day.
Member checks: {"earlier_end_check":"No earlier end found. The only cessation source is the order's own exemption period, \"a period of one year ending October 31, 1942\". The packet's bounded number search of the held order texts (lookup scope: exactly held_texts(), clause-drafts excluded) found no other held text that cites Executive Order 8844. The Federal Register catalog disposition-note search (later-events:ofr_catalog, checked 2026-10-19) returned no hit for this order. The person-name search of the held order texts (namier:held_texts_person_name, checked 2026-10-28, whose leads list later or undated orders only) found no later order naming Harry T. Edwards; its record lists only earlier orders for him (EO 8259, EO 8525 and EO 8827). Statute check (the type rule research/readiness/type-rules/TR-RETIREMENT-EXEMPTION.json lists a change in the governing retirement statute as a possible earlier end): held APP exemption orders signed after this one still rest on the same section, for example EO 9271 of November 17, 1942 (\"section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), I hereby further exempt the said George T. Summerlin from compulsory retirement for age\") and EO 9530 of March 14, 1945 (\"section 204 of the act of June 30, 1932, 47 Stat. 382, 404 (U.S.C., title 5, sec. 715a), I hereby exempt the said Harry F. Hawley from compulsory retirement for age\"). Their wording shows that section 204 was still the authority for exemptions after this order's last day, so it had not been repealed. No held source shows that any amendment of section 204 extended or extinguished this order's dated exemption, and a later general rule is an end only with a reason it extinguished the command (owner ruling 2026-10-28). The employee's separation, retirement or death would end the practical use of the exemption but is not an instrument ending the command, and none is cited in the packet. Later amendment: none found.","independent_command_check":"None found. The order contains one command, the amendment of EO 8827's period that fixes Edwards's exemption through October 31, 1942; it prescribes no report, procedure or continuing duty. The compulsory-retirement rule it excepts comes from section 204 and the retirement statutes, which are independent of this order.","savings_check":"The exemption this order fixes is exhausted at 1942-10-31; its accrued effects (service, pay, retirement credit) are separate from the order and are stated as transferred_or_accrued. No later exemption order for the same person is found by the name search.","scope_check":"Scope: one named employee, Harry T. Edwards (senior technologist, Bureau of Plant Industry, Department of Agriculture). The dossier inventory has one whole-order clause (characters 0-522 of the registered file, the whole amending sentence); the signature and date line follow it in the full 558-character text. Nothing in the text is outside that clause, so no inventory correction is needed."}
